---
/**
 * Who runs this site, what it claims, and what stands in place of a credential.
 *
 * The argument is deliberate. A page about retirement money with no named author is
 * worth nothing, and a page that invents a credential, a review board or a "reviewed
 * by" line is worth less than nothing, because a reader who catches one invention has
 * to throw out everything else too. What this project has instead is a public
 * repository, specifications frozen before their results were seen, a ledger that
 * records the failures, and a corrections log. Weaker than a licence, and checkable.
 *
 * Counts come from `uv run python -m portfolio_edge.reporting.programme_status`, which
 * reads the ledger. Re-read it before editing a number here.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "~/lib/site";
---

<Base
  title="About this site and the person who writes it"
  description="Sean Flanagan writes this. He holds no financial licence and no professional credential, the site sells nothing, and what stands in place of a credential is a public repository, frozen specifications, a ledger of failed attempts and a corrections log."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>About</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">About this site and the person who writes it</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        Who wrote this, and why believe any of it? Checked against <code>docs/research/</code> on
        <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>One person built this. His name is Sean Flanagan, he holds no financial licence or credential of any kind,
    and you should know that before the first number.</p>

    <p>What you are reading is the reading half of a public repository. The measuring half is Python:
    <span data-numeric>23</span> experiment families, <span data-numeric>27</span> frozen specifications,
    <span data-numeric>47</span> runs and a ledger with <span data-numeric>134</span> entries in it. Every number
    on these pages carries a link back to the file that owns it. If the link goes somewhere that does not say what
    the sentence says, the sentence is wrong, and an <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`} rel="noopener noreferrer">issue
    on GitHub</a> is the fastest way to say so.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The author</Rail>

    <h2>Who writes this</h2>

    <MarginNote label="No badge">
      <p>
        There is no review board, no editorial panel, and no "reviewed by" line. A site that claims a credential
        it does not hold has told you what its other claims are worth.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Sean Flanagan. A software engineer in the United States, writing in his own time, with no employer or
    client anywhere near this work.</p>

    <p>He is not a registered investment adviser. He does not work for a broker-dealer or a registered investment
    advisory firm. He is not a financial planner and holds no CFA, CFP, CPA, Series 7 or Series 65. No licence, no
    firm, no professional body, and nobody reviewing a page before it goes up.</p>

    <p>That last sentence is the one this page exists to say plainly. The temptation on a site like this is to
    invent a review board or borrow an institution's shape, and it would be the cheapest thing here to fake. It is
    also the most expensive thing to be caught at, because a reader who finds one invented credential is right to
    discard the other four hundred numbers on the site.</p>

    <p>Large parts of this were drafted with AI assistance, which is true of the code, the research prose and
    these pages. <a href="/methodology/">How a result earns its status</a> says exactly what was machine-written,
    what was checked by hand, and what none of that protects you from.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The argument</Rail>

    <h2>What stands in place of a credential</h2>

    <p>Shown work, and a great deal of it.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Where to look">
      <p>
        The specifications are under <code>research/specs/</code>, the ledger is
        <code>research/ledger.jsonl</code>, and the syntheses are <code>docs/research/</code>. All of it is in the
        same repository as the site you are reading.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <ul>
      <li>The <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">repository is public</a>, every commit of it, including
      the ones that undo the previous week.</li>
      <li>A specification is frozen before anyone sees the result it will produce, which is what stops a bar from
      being moved to meet a number. There are <span data-numeric>27</span> of them.</li>
      <li>The ledger records every attempt that carried a hypothesis, including the abandoned ones and the ones
      that failed. Nine runs ended in a rejection and nine ended in "we could not tell".</li>
      <li>Thirty-three errors have been found in this project's own published numbers. Each one is on
      <a href="/corrections/">the corrections log</a> with its date, what caught it, and what changed as a
      result.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>You cannot audit a certificate. You can audit a diff.</p>

    <p>None of that makes a conclusion here correct. It makes each one checkable by somebody who wants to argue,
    which is the only offer anyone in this position can honestly make.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Money</Rail>

    <h2>How it is paid for</h2>

    <p>It isn't, really.</p>

    <p>No advertising. No affiliate links. No sponsors, no paid placement, no compensation from any fund company,
    broker, custodian or data provider. Nothing to subscribe to and nothing to buy. Hosting a static site costs a
    few dollars a month and comes out of the author's own pocket.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Where the interests are listed">
      <p>
        What the author owns is not published. <a href="/disclosures/#holdings">The disclosures page</a> says so
        plainly, and lists the interests that do exist.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>One thing is better said than left to inference. A site with nothing to sell can still be wrong. Having
    no advertiser and no product removes one failure mode out of many, and the rest of them are still live.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Scope</Rail>

    <h2>What the site claims, and what it refuses to</h2>

    <p>Three sentences, in the order the site is built in.</p>

    <p>The reliable win is fees, taxes and which account each fund sits in, worth roughly 90 to 110 basis points a
    year against the portfolio an ordinary investor would otherwise hold. It is close to certain, it needs no
    forecast, and your own account would show it inside a year.</p>

    <p>Beating a cheap index fund is a different question, and the honest answer is that we cannot demonstrate it:
    about <span data-numeric>5</span> basis points a year of defensible advantage against
    <span data-numeric>313</span> basis points of year-to-year drift away from that index. Saying so on the fourth
    screen rather than the fortieth is most of what makes this site different from the ones it is competing
    with.</p>

    <p>Everything after that is mostly "we can't tell". That is a verdict rather than an evasion, and
    <a href="/how-sure/">how sure we are</a> explains the difference.</p>

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>
        Nothing here has been checked by a securities lawyer, a tax adviser or an accountant. The tax arithmetic
        assumes a US federal individual investor and ignores every state, and a US-specific tax result is often
        exactly backwards somewhere else.
      </p>
    </Callout>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The line</Rail>

    <h2>The one thing this site will never do</h2>

    <p>It will never answer a question about your portfolio.</p>

    <p>Not by email, not in a GitHub issue, not in a comment, not privately. Half of that is a legal condition and
    <a href="/disclaimer/">the disclaimer</a> explains which half. The other half is honest: every number here was
    measured against a reference case, and nobody here knows your tax bracket, your job security, what you owe,
    when you stop working, or what you actually did in March 2020. A general answer wearing a personal answer's
    clothes is worse than no answer, because you would act on it.</p>

    <p>Questions about the method, the data or an error in a number are a different thing entirely, and those are
    welcome anywhere.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>A note on grammar</Rail>

    <h2>Why the site says "we"</h2>

    <p>There is one person here, and almost every page says "we" anyway.</p>

    <p>"We" means the research programme rather than a team: the frozen specifications, the runs, the code, the
    tests and the reviews that argued with each other and occasionally won. "I" would be more accurate about who
    typed the words and less accurate about what produced the number. The pronoun stays, and this paragraph is
    where the site tells you it is one person, which seemed better than four hundred sentences opening "I
    measured".</p>

    <p>That is the whole disclosure. The rest of the site is the work.</p>

  </div>
</Base>
